And from here, I create a stacked column plot. Each column is assigned an area and within each area you have the different categories:

a <- hPlot(Listens ~ Areas,
                 data = CatplotData,
                 type = 'column',
                 group = 'Categories',
                 group.na = 'NA\'s')

Add any other parameters to customize your plot ...

Then, this is my JS code to track when the user clicks on any of the categories on any of the areas:

a$plotOptions(column = list(stacking = "normal",
                                  cursor = 'pointer',
                                  point = list(events = list(click =
                                                             "#! function() {
                                                             Shiny.onInputChange('click', {
                                                             topic: this.name <http://this.name> + '-' + this.series.name <http://this.series.name>
                                                           })
                                                           } !#"))))

In the R code I just:

observe ({
   input$click$topic

   #add whatever logic you want execute

})

I hope this helps.

Carlos

